import Sql from '../../components/explainers/querying/Sql.astro';
import RecoveryFigure from '../../components/relational-next/a22/RecoveryFigure.astro';
import rehearsalUrl from '../../components/relational-next/a22/rehearse.mjs?url&no-inline';
const schema=`CREATE TABLE order_operations (
  tenant_id bigint NOT NULL,
  customer_id bigint NOT NULL,
  kind text NOT NULL,
  request_key text NOT NULL,
  fingerprint text NOT NULL,
  order_id uuid REFERENCES orders(order_id)
    DEFERRABLE INITIALLY DEFERRED,
  result jsonb NOT NULL,
  PRIMARY KEY (tenant_id, customer_id, kind, request_key)
);`;
const claim=`BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL READ COMMITTED;
SET LOCAL lock_timeout = '100ms'; -- illustrative policy

INSERT INTO order_operations
  (tenant_id, customer_id, kind, request_key,
   fingerprint, order_id, result)
VALUES ($1, $2, 'place-order', $3, $4, $5, $6)
ON CONFLICT (tenant_id, customer_id, kind, request_key)
DO NOTHING
RETURNING order_id;

-- If one row returned: this transaction owns the claim.
-- Make the business changes and finalise the retained result.
-- COMMIT only when both are complete.

-- If zero rows returned: a SEPARATE next statement reads
-- the retained fingerprint and result under the same scope.
-- Compare the fingerprint; do not repeat the business change.
-- End this read-only duplicate attempt before responding.`;
---

<p>A request can fail to return an answer even though its database transaction committed. Repeating the request blindly can then place a second order. Refusing every repeat leaves the customer unable to discover the first one. Safe recovery needs evidence about the transaction and a durable identity for the operation the customer intended.</p>
<p>Mina is ordering a mug and two packets of tea, the request from <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/">Building applications around a relational database</a>. The API reserves stock, records the order and returns its identifier. Here the ordinary successful path is already understood; the next request arrives after a timeout, a conflict or a missing response. PostgreSQL 18.6 and node-postgres <code>pg</code> 8.23.1 provide the concrete examples.</p>

